\caption(The second edge saturates. The final complete BFS reaches vertices 0 and 1, producing a cut whose capacity equals the feasible flow value.)
\alttext(A native three-node directed chain shows flow two on capacities three and two. The first two nodes are green, and the saturated final edge crossing to the orange sink is red.)

> Derive residual augmentation

For each original input edge e=(u,v) of capacity c_e, keep its flow
f_e with 0<=f_e<=c_e. At every vertex except the terminals,

\[
\sum_{e\text{ leaving }v}f_e-\sum_{e\text{ entering }v}f_e=0.
\]

The source's net outgoing flow is the objective value. Every input
edge owns a forward residual arc of capacity c_e−f_e and a reverse
cancellation arc of capacity f_e. Parallel edges keep separate pairs;
an original v→u edge is distinct from the cancellation arc of u→v.
A self edge does not increase source-to-sink value and is never part
of a simple augmenting path.

BFS traverses strictly positive residual arcs. Its parent chain to the
sink is a shortest path by edge count. Let delta be the minimum used
residual. Decrease each used residual by delta and increase its paired
opposite by delta. A forward use increases the original flow; a reverse
use decreases it. Internal path vertices gain and lose equal amounts,
so conservation remains true, while the source value increases by delta.
The bottleneck ensures capacity bounds remain satisfied in exact arithmetic.

The kernel completes every BFS, even after discovering the sink. This
keeps all actual distances and first-discovery parent arc ids available.
It preserves the original first sink path because the graph does not
change during a search. Oriented ids 2e and 2e+1 identify an input
edge's forward and reverse arcs; SIZE_MAX means no parent. Unreachable
distance is V. The final failed search directly supplies the cut, with
no uncounted second reachability traversal.

If S is the final reachable set, no positive residual crosses from S
to its complement. Every original S→complement edge is therefore
saturated, and every complement→S edge has zero flow: otherwise its
cancellation arc would leave S. Summing conservation over S gives
flow value equal to outgoing cut capacity. Any feasible flow is bounded
above by any separating cut, so these matching witnesses establish
optimality in exact arithmetic. The numerical certificate reports
feasibility errors and the signed duality gap separately; nonzero
tolerance would mean numerical agreement, rather than an exact proof.

> An actual cancellation repairs an earlier choice

Interpret the following unit-capacity network as a small matching
problem: source 0 connects to left vertices 1 and 2; right vertices
3 and 4 connect to sink 5. Available middle edges are 1→3, 1→4
and 2→3. The first shortest path uses 1→3. To send a second unit,
BFS follows 0→2→3→1→4→5. The step 3→1 is the cancellation
arc of input edge e2, undoing the earlier 1→3 flow. Final matches
are 1→4 and 2→3. No additional matching implementation is needed;
the report reads the actual residual path records.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edmonds_karp_cancellation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::tuple<std::size_t,std::size_t,double>> edges{{0,1,1},{0,2,1},{1,3,1},{1,4,1},{2,3,1},{3,5,1},{4,5,1}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace(6,edges,0,5);
  std::print("Cancellation: flow {}; searches {}; inspections {}; path updates {}; captured entries {}.\n\n",t.result.max_flow,t.work.bfs_calls,t.work.bfs_arc_inspections,t.work.path_arc_updates,t.captured_entries);
  std::print("| push | amount | input edge | orientation | from | to | used before | used after | paired before | paired after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.2;box.height=1.2;box.circle=true;box.font_size=23;
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<t.augmentations.size();++k){const auto& a=t.augmentations[k];const double offset=k==0?3.8:0,y=-4.*k;
    d.text({9.5,y+1.6},std::format("Push {}: amount {}, shortest path has {} edges",k,a.amount,a.path.size()),22);
    d.node(std::format("{}-0",k),{offset,y},std::to_string(a.path.front().from),box);
    for(std::size_t j=0;j<a.path.size();++j){const auto& arc=a.path[j];std::print("| {} | {} | e{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,a.amount,arc.input_edge,arc.reverse?"reverse":"forward",arc.from,arc.to,arc.residual_before,arc.residual_after,arc.paired_before,arc.paired_after);
      d.node(std::format("{}-{}",k,j+1),{offset+3.8*(j+1),y},std::to_string(arc.to),box);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke line;if(arc.reverse)line.color={220,38,38};
      d.edge(std::format("{}-{}",k,j),std::format("{}-{}",k,j+1),std::format("e{} {}",arc.input_edge,arc.reverse?"reverse":"forward"),line);}}
  const auto& undo=t.augmentations.at(1).path.at(2);
  if(!undo.reverse||undo.input_edge!=2||t.result.edge_flow.at(2)!=0)throw std::runtime_error("missing actual cancellation");
  std::print("\nActual reverse use: e{}, {} to {}; final original e2 flow {}.\n",undo.input_edge,undo.from,undo.to,t.result.edge_flow.at(2));
  d.text({9.5,3.2},"The second shortest path cancels the first middle-edge choice",25).save_svg("build/edmonds_karp_cancellation.svg");
}
