> Boundary semantics, numerical progress and owned history

All capacities must be finite and nonnegative, all endpoints in range,
and terminals distinct and valid. Complete validation and checked
residual/vector geometry precede adjacency allocation. Strictly positive
residuals include subnormal capacities; no arbitrary epsilon discards them.
Parallel and antiparallel edges retain their own pairs, while self edges
remain unused by a simple source-to-sink path.

Double arithmetic cannot represent every update. Each used residual
must strictly decrease, each paired residual and the total must strictly
increase, and sums must remain finite. A lost positive update raises an
error; overflow raises an error. The original code accepted NaN/infinite
capacities and recovered edge flow by subtracting nearly equal capacity
and forward residual values. A saved probe with capacities 1e300 and
unit bottlenecks reported total two but edge flows 0,1,1,0, violating
internal conservation. This implementation returns no partial result
when the required residual update cannot be represented. Recovering
successful per-edge flows from cancellation residuals also avoids that
nearly-equal subtraction. This is not arbitrary-precision arithmetic.

The trace counts E original input records and one network header;
each of P complete searches costs one header plus V levels and V
parents; each of A pushes costs one header plus its path-arc records.
Writing L for total path length,

\[
C=E+1+P(1+2V)+A+L.
\]

The minimum including the first BFS is checked before input copying or
adjacency allocation. Later search/path costs are reserved before their
traversal or push. The default cap is one million entries; zero disables
only the optional quota. Result copies, working arrays, scalar metadata,
vector capacity and allocator bytes are excluded, so C is not a
peak-memory bound. Quota or arithmetic failure returns no partial trace.
Ordinary/work calls retain no optional history and have no optional quota.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/edmonds_karp_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const dm::algorithms::FlowNetwork g{2,{{0,0,7},{0,1,1.5},{0,1,2.5},{1,0,9},{1,1,5}},0,1};
  const auto r=dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(g.vertex_count,g.edges,g.source,g.sink);
  std::print("Separate identities: total {}; per-input flows:",r.max_flow);for(auto f:r.edge_flow)std::print(" {}",f);std::print(".\n");
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace(2,{{0,1,3}},0,1,14);int caps=0;
  for(std::size_t cap:{6,13})try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace(2,{{0,1,3}},0,1,cap);}catch(const std::length_error&){++caps;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace(100000000,{},0,1,1);}catch(const std::length_error&){++caps;}
  std::print("Trace minimum {}; exact entries {}; disabled-cap entries {}; minimum/one-short/large-geometry rejections {}/3.\n",dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace_base_entries(2,1),exact.captured_entries,dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp_trace(2,{{0,1,3}},0,1,0).captured_entries,caps);
  const double inf=std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();int invalid=0;
  for(double bad:{-1.,inf,-inf,std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()})try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(4,{{2,3,bad}},0,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++invalid;}
  int lost=0,overflow=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(2,{{0,1,1e300},{0,1,1}},0,1);}catch(const std::runtime_error&){++lost;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(4,{{0,1,1e300},{1,3,1},{0,2,1},{2,3,1e300}},0,3);}catch(const std::runtime_error&){++lost;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(2,{{0,1,1e308},{0,1,1e308}},0,1);}catch(const std::overflow_error&){++overflow;}
  std::print("Invalid capacity rejections {}/4; lost-progress rejections {}/2; overflow rejections {}/1.\n",invalid,lost,overflow);
  const double tiny=std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(),big=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  std::print("Smallest positive capacity preserved {}; largest finite single capacity preserved {}.\n",dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(2,{{0,1,tiny}},0,1).max_flow==tiny,dm::algorithms::edmonds_karp(2,{{0,1,big}},0,1).max_flow==big);
  auto d=dm::algorithms::plot_max_flow(g,r,{{0,0},{8,0}});
  d.text({4,3.8},"Separate self, parallel and antiparallel input edges",25).text({4,-2.5},"Only the two source-to-sink inputs carry flow: 1.5 + 2.5 = 4",20).save_svg("build/edmonds_karp_boundaries.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Separate identities: total 4; per-input flows: 0 1.5 2.5 0 0.
Trace minimum 7; exact entries 14; disabled-cap entries 14; minimum/one-short/large-geometry rejections 3/3.
Invalid capacity rejections 4/4; lost-progress rejections 2/2; overflow rejections 1/1.
Smallest positive capacity preserved true; largest finite single capacity preserved true.
\image(figures/edmonds_karp_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Self edges and the opposite original input carry zero; the two parallel forward inputs independently carry 1.5 and 2.5. The capture and numerical controls above fail explicitly.)
\alttext(A native two-node diagram shows separate self loops, two forward lanes and an opposite lane, each labelled with its original edge identity and flow/capacity.)

The drawing helper validates finite shapes, cut ids and node positions,
but does not certify arbitrary caller-edited flows or cuts. The independent
certificate API is the appropriate place to inspect feasibility and gaps.
Owned traces can also be edited; their records are evidence from a run,
rather than a self-validating proof object.

> What the examples establish

Shortest residual paths expose both the bottleneck and the cancellations
needed to repair earlier routing. The original flow of 23 has matching
primal and cut witnesses, while complete BFS evidence and controlled
parallel edges make operation counts explicit. Checked finite progress
and retained-history limits describe the implementation's practical
boundary; they do not replace the exact-arithmetic theorem with an
unqualified numerical guarantee.

> References

- J. Edmonds and R. M. Karp (1972), “Theoretical Improvements in
  Algorithmic Efficiency for Network Flow Problems”, Journal of the ACM
  19(2), 248–264, [doi:10.1145/321694.321699](https://doi.org/10.1145/321694.321699).
  [Original paper](https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~pettie/matching/Edmonds-Karp-network-flow.pdf).
  The implementation uses shortest residual paths with independent input
  edge pairs, complete BFS capture and explicit checks on double updates.
- L. R. Ford Jr. and D. R. Fulkerson (1956), “Maximal flow through a
  network”, Canadian Journal of Mathematics 8, 399–404,
  [doi:10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5](https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5).
  Flow/cut duality supplies the certificate interpretation.
- The six-vertex, ten-edge CLRS fixture is retained from the original
  report's example of Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest and Stein, Introduction
  to Algorithms, Figure 26.1. All capacities and computed outputs are
  included here, so the example can be checked without an external file.
